WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
[no sound]

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.348
[music playing]

00:00:08.348 --> 00:00:10.408
William Wang: This performance with the Pops
is

00:00:10.408 --> 00:00:13.334
probably one of the most 
exciting ones that I've done.

00:00:13.334 --> 00:00:16.843
It's my first time playing with 
a professional orchestra.

00:00:16.843 --> 00:00:21.885
[music playing]

00:00:21.885 --> 00:00:28.124
Wang: The focus of my PhD is machine learning
and statistics and I'm investigating theoretical

00:00:28.124 --> 00:00:34.076
questions, like how much data do you need
in order to actually make a good decision.

00:00:34.076 --> 00:00:38.850
It's great having music as an outlet to just
be able to do something in your free time

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:43.829
and to just kind of switch your mind out of
research and focus on something different

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:46.320
and activate different areas of your brain.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.039
I think my younger self would be elated to
hear that I was playing with the Pops because

00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:55.600
I would always watch a YouTube recording of
their Stars and Stripes Forever performance.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.512
And I was always inspired by that.

00:00:57.512 --> 00:01:00.267
I would just watch that every 
4th of July so… [laughs]

00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:05.327
[music playing]

00:01:05.327 --> 00:01:10.140
Wang: When you come into the practice rooms
at MIT, you're supposed to disinfect the keys

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.995
before and after you practice.

00:01:12.995 --> 00:01:15.970
And then it just makes a sound like... it
just sounds like this.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.234
[random notes from piano keys]

00:01:18.234 --> 00:01:21.071
So you just hear that in the practice 
rooms a lot when people

00:01:21.071 --> 00:01:23.234
are coming in and leaving to practice.

00:01:23.234 --> 00:01:29.167
[piano keys playing in lower octave]

00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:30.600
[random notes from piano keys]

00:01:31.571 --> 00:01:33.734
[music playing]

00:01:33.734 --> 00:01:39.326
I gave a recital recently that had just a
few members of a live audience.

00:01:39.326 --> 00:01:41.369
I had to wear a mask 
when I was performing.

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:43.770
The rest of the people watched it on Zoom.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:47.833
So it was nice because I could have 
my friends in California watch.

00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:53.456
[music playing]

00:01:53.456 --> 00:01:56.067
[music changes]

00:01:56.067 --> 00:02:00.046
Wang: So I chose to play the 
Rachmaninoff concerto because, 

00:02:00.046 --> 00:02:02.284
first of all, it's a great piece of music.

00:02:02.284 --> 00:02:07.659
And it's just also very close to my heart
because I've been playing it for quite a while.

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:10.470
I learned it four years ago.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:14.207
I do like the first movement for 
its beautiful lyrical themes, but 

00:02:14.207 --> 00:02:18.660
there was some also 
deeper angst, I guess.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.485
The solo sections of this 
piece are very intimate.

00:02:22.485 --> 00:02:26.660
And there's a lot of beauty in them.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:32.558
They're very passionate and loving, but it's
also kind of sad and bittersweet.

00:02:32.558 --> 00:02:34.273
[music playing]

00:02:34.273 --> 00:02:36.673
I guess in a time of pandemic, 
sometimes you don't want to 

00:02:36.673 --> 00:02:39.592
plunge into something completely new.

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:43.800
Maybe different people have different ways
of coping, but I like to go back to what I

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.633
originally liked and rediscover 
what I liked about it.

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:52.541
[music playing]

00:02:52.541 --> 00:02:56.200
Wang: I think one of the most important things
about music is that you can kind of take away

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.620
what you want to take away from it.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.010
There's a lot of interpretive history that
different people play it different ways.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:07.540
I hope that my particular way of playing resonates
in you and somehow brings you joy.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:11.612
[music concludes]

00:03:11.612 --> 00:03:22.766
[no sound]

